WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.751
Jason Mewes is taking the spotlight again
in 2019 with Jay and Silent Bob Reboot, as

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.580
well as his directorial debut, Madness in
the Method.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:12.640
But it hasn't always been lighthearted fun
in the life of this actor, filmmaker, and

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:13.140
podcaster.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:17.440
His rise and fall and rise again is a remarkable
story, and he was kind enough to tell us all

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:17.940
about it.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:24.480
"Kal son of Jor-El - Kneel before Zod!

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:25.880
Snuchie poochies!"

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.660
While Mewes cracked audiences up with his
drug-related antics in the movies of hometown

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:35.430
friend Kevin Smith, he spent what should have
been the best years of his career falling

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.080
increasingly into addiction.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:42.200
Even as he shined on-screen, substance abuse
was quietly causing damage in his life, endangering

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.120
his safety and derailing his career.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:49.260
Fortunately, Mewes was able to turn the tables
on his addictions in the early 2000s, starting

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:52.160
a process of sober living that he's continued
to this day.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.660
According to Smith, the Silent Bob to Mewes'
Jay, Mewes was

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.820
"...born the son of a heroin abuser, [and]
spent most of his childhood raised by an aunt

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.540
while his Mother fed her jones or spent years
in jail."

00:01:02.809 --> 00:01:06.640
Despite this, Mewes was essentially living
a straight-edge lifestyle when Smith first

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.340
met him, free of booze, drugs, and even romance.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:13.040
Things can change pretty fast when you're
a teenager, though, and when the time came

00:01:13.049 --> 00:01:17.820
to shoot Clerks, Mewes had morphed into an
enthusiastic drinker and pot smoker, and essentially

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:18.980
played himself in the movie.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:21.440
"Here is a dude who was like "I dunno if I
can say these things.'

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:22.820
And I was like 'dude, you do say these things!'"

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.040
Yeah you c---smokin clerk.

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:28.500
And how many times have I told you not to
be dealing in front of the store!

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:30.500
I'm not dealing man what are you talking about?

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:31.500
“Hey you got anything man?”

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:32.020
“Yeah, what you want?"

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:36.480
But while the scumbag lifestyle came off as
funny on-screen, the real-life Mewes was on

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:37.480
a dangerous path.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:41.140
The success of Clerks left Mewes living a
kind of dual life.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.860
He had a foot in the film industry, but wasn't
making or saving a ton of money.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.680
When filming wrapped, he'd go back to working
regular jobs.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:53.859
I came home from Mallrats and started delivering
pizza and doing construction with my buddy.

00:01:53.859 --> 00:01:59.290
[...] And then I did Chasing Amy, and I went
back to delivering pizza and doing construction

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:00.290
and stuff.

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:01.640
I worked a regular job for years.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:06.960
It really wasn't until after Dogma that I
stopped working construction and delivering

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:07.460
pizza."

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:12.360
But as Mewes' star continued to rise, his
drug use was becoming more problematic, especially

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:17.150
as he shifted away from weed and beers toward
more addictive substances heroin, cocaine,

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:18.140
and even crack.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:22.300
When Mewes began shooting heroin, Smith began
working to get Mewes off the opiates.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.760
He gave him rides to methadone clinics, where
the recognizable duo drew stares as Smith

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:27.620
would later put it,

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.120
"Stoners are cute; junkies are sad."

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:34.200
You think just because he just don't say anything
it'll have some huge impact when he does open

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:34.700
his --"

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:35.700
"J---- Christ, why don't you shut up."

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:40.180
Mewes is clear that it wasn't Hollywood pressure
that made him dip further into using drugs.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.340
"I started using drugs in Jersey.

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:45.900
It definitely isn't a Hollywood thing.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:48.870
It definitely isn't a movie thing, or anything.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:54.840
I definitely I definitely feel I've started
drinking, if anything, more, but that was

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:58.920
more because I wanted to go out, and there's
lots of fun clubs."

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.540
As Mewes put it, in the film industry,

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.500
"Opiates and heroin and painkillers and stuff
is taboo"

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.540
which allowed Mewes to keep the extent of
his drug use private.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:10.640
On screen, he was serving up the goods, and
if he was falling asleep on set in the middle

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.720
of takes while shooting Dogma well, audiences
didn't notice.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:16.680
"You can't just go around breaking people's
hearts like that?

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.140
I fell in love with you!

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:19.980
We fell in love with you!

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.940
Guys like us don't just fall out of the f------
sky you know!"

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:27.500
According to Kevin Smith, Mewes' drug usage
had reached dangerous, friendship-testing

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:29.360
levels by the time Dogma wrapped.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:33.680
On top of nodding off on set, he was acquiring
track marks and damaging his teeth.

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:37.740
He once reportedly rolled his truck through
a traffic stop while under the influence,

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.360
colliding with the car of an off-duty police
officer.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:44.730
He was arrested for heroin possession in New
Jersey after being stopped by police for driving

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:49.170
around with a deployed airbag, an incident
which was later used for yuks in a scene in

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:50.160
Clerks II.

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:52.420
"Driving around with a deployed airbag."

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.560
After Dogma, circumstances would occasionally
compel Mewes to check into rehab programs,

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:59.820
leaving him off drugs for brief periods of
time.

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:04.240
But the drugs remained a fixation, to the
point where his next movie almost didn't happen.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:08.400
During the pre-production of Jay and Silent
Bob Strike Back, a blow-up between Mewes and

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:11.460
Smith nearly resulted in the movie being canceled
in pre-production.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.620
As Kevin Smith tells the story, it would be
better to cut their losses than risk having

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:18.200
Mewes fall off the wagon while the movie was
being shot.

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:20.369
The director recalls that he told Mewes,

00:04:20.369 --> 00:04:23.820
"You can do all the f------ dope you want
now, because we're stopping the movie."

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:28.180
For fans of the stoner duo, the whole episode
is a bracing dose of reality.

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:30.080
While the movie may have been lighthearted,

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:31.420
"why do they call you C---nocker?”

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.700
“Actually there's a funny story behind that.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:36.940
You're gonna love this true story..."

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:41.780
the backdrop of its production was not.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:45.980
According to Smith, Mewes stayed clean during
shooting, but was back to using opiates within

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:47.700
hours after shooting his last scene.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.879
During the months of post-production while
Smith was busy finishing the movie, his on-screen

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:55.320
partner reportedly lost 30 pounds as a result
of smoking crack.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.480
After resuming his drug usage, Mewes was caught
having heroin mailed to a hotel during a Strike

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.740
Back press junket, and was also caught stealing
money from Smith.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:07.940
In New Jersey, a warrant was issued for his
arrest after he failed to show up for a court

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:11.220
date, violating the probation he was on after
his previous arrest.

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:16.419
All of these factors led Smith to decide against
casting Mewes in his sixth film, Jersey Girl,

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:19.640
which was mostly shot around their hometown
of Highlands, New Jersey.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:23.960
Shut out of production and knowing he'd be
arrested if he ever returned home, Mewes decided

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:26.120
to face his problems head-on and surrender.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:31.120
Mewes' return to New Jersey to face the authorities
was accompanied by an all-time indicator of

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:34.800
a man living on the edge a short-lived rumor
that he had died.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:39.360
Instead of being locked away immediately,
Mewes was given a choice: a year in jail,

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:40.470
or six months of rehab.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:41.620
He chose rehab.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:46.420
"It wasn't like I stepped away from everything
to be like, 'Hey, I need to clear my head

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:51.600
from being in the Hollywood spotlight, or
on the town or something.'

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:56.260
It was literally just like, 'Hey, I need to
try to take care of this problem I have, and

00:05:56.270 --> 00:06:02.990
I don't know how to do it besides going to
turn myself in to the to the New Jersey court

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:05.099
systems,' or what have you.

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:07.240
And I just fell off the map."

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:11.360
It turns out, falling off the map was exactly
the thing he needed to turn things around.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:16.560
More than four years into Mewes' efforts at
going sober, he fell victim to a common pitfall

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:20.460
feared by many recovering heroin users: legally-prescribed
opiates.

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:22.940
"It's not even like I went out and chased
it.

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:30.750
I had gotten hurt, and I got a shot of Demerol
for the pain, and that sparked the whole thing

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:31.240
back.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:33.700
'Oh, I remember that feeling and I miss it.'

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:37.240
I relapsed again, and Kevin was like, 'You
were doing so well.

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:39.300
You had over four years.

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:40.600
What happened?'

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:42.720
[...] I wasn't accountable to anybody or talking
to anyone.'"

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:46.260
Mewes realized he needed to be accountable
to something more than just himself.

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:50.440
Talking to Smith about his struggles, the
idea came up to essentially keep the conversation

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:53.020
going indefinitely, and invite the public
to listen in.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:54.960
"I wanted to do a podcast.

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:58.119
And he was like what do you want to do the
podcast about?

00:06:58.119 --> 00:07:03.099
And I was like, 'Why don't we talk about what
you're thinking every day, and this way you're

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:06.280
accountable to people who are listening and
watching.'"

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:11.300
The ensuing podcast, Jay and Silent Bob Get
Old, went on to produce over 200 episodes.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:15.699
According to Mewes, the show is over, at least
for now, giving way to the obligations of

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:19.280
the touring Reboot Roadshow as well as Mewes'
own Twitch channel.

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:22.360
"Oh, yes, you snake, ha!

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:23.800
Yes, baby!”

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:29.099
For Mewes, the podcast was a constant reminder
of the commitment he'd made to sobriety, and

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:32.380
a way to connect with fans all around the
world going through the same struggles.

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:36.129
Much like the movies he's most famous for,
it was an experience that brought moments

00:07:36.129 --> 00:07:38.469
both profane and surprisingly profound.

00:07:38.469 --> 00:07:43.289
"Who would've thunk that we literally went
seven years, and we toured Australia and London

00:07:43.289 --> 00:07:44.120
and all that stuff.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:45.920
And that was Jay and Silent Bob Get Old."

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:50.659
Outside of the podcast and keeping busy with
work, Mewes says he makes his continuing sobriety

00:07:50.659 --> 00:07:54.879
work with the help of a caring support system,
which includes his family, and friends who

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:56.569
aren't shy about keeping him accountable.

00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:02.160
"A friend who's like, 'Hey, you've been sober
two years and you're really addicted to drugs,

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:02.840
not booze.

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:04.020
You can have a beer.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:05.160
You can have one beer.'

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:05.880
That to me isn't…

00:08:06.169 --> 00:08:09.540
again, he might be a friend, but not really,"

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:11.749
"So that to me was a big deal.

00:08:11.749 --> 00:08:16.389
Surrounding myself with people who knew, no,
I can't have a beer, because then I'll be

00:08:16.389 --> 00:08:20.219
smoking crack in a month, and I'll disappear."

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:24.380
Whatever he's doing, it seems to be working,
and he's been sober for more than nine years.

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:28.919
"you know, sometimes I wish I did more in
my life instead of just hanging out in front

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:30.880
of places and selling weed and s---.”

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:38.320
On top of closing out 2019 by touring with
the Reboot Roadshow, Mewes also celebrated

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:41.840
a major milestone in his career directing
his first movie.

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:47.020
The film, Madness in the Method, was released
in theaters and on-demand in August 2019.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:51.310
The premise of the movie revolves around a
world much like the real one, with Mewes playing

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:52.960
a twisted version of himself.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:58.200
"Everyone plays themselves, but like a multiverse
version of themselves."

00:08:58.210 --> 00:09:01.660
The movie follows Jay in as he attempts to
break out of the more stoner-style roles he's

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:03.360
had a tendency to be typecast in.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:08.279
Less true to life is how he accomplishes this
goal, following the unorthodox advice from

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:12.100
a mysterious book on method acting, to unexpectedly
effective results.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:16.040
Stepping into the role of director didn't
just fulfill a longtime goal for Mewes.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:19.620
It also demonstrated just how many of his
peers in the film industry have come to care

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:21.709
about him over his long and charmed career.

00:09:21.709 --> 00:09:27.150
"It was really nice, because when we reached
out to Dean Cain, Teri Hatcher, Jaime Camil,

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:28.550
everyone like, 'Hey, listen.

00:09:28.550 --> 00:09:32.569
We don't have a lot of money but Jay Mewes
is directing his first movie.

00:09:32.569 --> 00:09:33.569
Is there any way?'

00:09:33.569 --> 00:09:39.550
And they came and was part of the movie because
they were like, 'We want to be part of that.

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:43.980
If he's going to be directing for the first
time, we want to be part of his directing

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:44.880
debut.'

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:46.440
So that was really, really nice."

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:48.660
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:51.980
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
stars are coming soon.

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:55.700
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

